  "judge_reasoning": "## Evaluation Analysis\n\n**Task Requirement:** Find the software requirements for iPhones that support AirDrop's ability to continue transmitting over the web when out of range.\n\n**Evidence from Screenshots:**\n\n1. **Screenshot 1 (Google Search):** The user entered a relevant search query: \"iPhone AirDrop continue transfer over internet when out of range software requirements iOS\" — directly aligned with the task.\n\n2. **Screenshot 2 (Google Results/AI Overview):** The AI Overview explicitly states: *\"To continue an AirDrop transfer over the internet/cellular data when moving out of range, you need **iOS 17.1 or later** on both the sending and receiving iPhones.\"* It also lists additional requirements (iCloud sign-in, initial connection within range, cellular setting enabled). The Apple Support result snippet also confirms: \"If you leave Bluetooth or Wi-Fi range after an AirDrop transfer is initiated, the transfer continues over the internet.\"\n\n3. **Screenshots 3 & 4 (Apple iPhone User Guide):** The official Apple Support page \"Use AirDrop to send items to nearby Apple devices\" confirms the feature: *\"Note: If you leave Bluetooth or Wi-Fi range after an AirDrop transfer is initiated, the transfer continues over the internet.\"*\n\n4. **Screenshot 5 (MacRumors):** The article title \"Apple Releases iOS 17.1 and iPadOS 17.1 With AirDrop Over Internet...\" confirms that the AirDrop over internet feature was introduced in iOS 17.1.\n\n**Assessment of the Response:**\n- The final answer \"iOS 17.1 or later\" is directly corroborated by the Google AI Overview and the MacRumors article title in the screenshots.\n- The claim about compatible devices (iPhone XS/XS Max/XR and later) is not visible in the screenshots, but per evaluation guidelines, content in the response not shown on the screenshot can be believed, and it is factually consistent with iOS 17.1 device compatibility.\n- The response correctly identifies the software requirement and provides supporting context from authoritative sources (Apple Support, MacRumors).\n\nThe task was fully accomplished — the correct software requirement (iOS 17.1 or later) was found and verified across multiple sources.\n\n## Verdict: **SUCCESS**",
